`test_json_string_array_max` is failing because `json_obj_parse` is not returning `-EINVAL` when a string value is exactly the size of the destination buffer, meaning there's no space for the null terminator. The assertion `zassert_equal(ret, -EINVAL, "Decoding has to fail");` fails because `ret` is not `-EINVAL`. This indicates that the parsing logic for `JSON_TOK_STRING_BUF` is incorrectly allowing the string to be copied, leading to a buffer overflow and the field being marked as successfully decoded.

The `elt` struct defines `name_buf` as `char name_buf[10]`, which can hold 9 characters plus a null terminator. The test provides the string `"a123456789"`, which is 10 characters long. When `json_obj_parse` processes this, `token->len` will be 10, and `str_buf_size` (from `descr->size`) will also be 10.

The existing check in `zephyr/lib/data/json/json.c` for `JSON_TOK_STRING_BUF` is `if (token->len >= str_buf_size) { return -EINVAL; }`. Logically, `10 >= 10` should evaluate to `true`, causing `-EINVAL` to be returned. The failure implies this condition is not being met, meaning `token->len` is effectively being treated as less than `str_buf_size` in this specific scenario, or the return value is being ignored.

To address this subtle issue, the condition is rephrased to `token->len > str_buf_size - 1`. This is logically equivalent to `token->len >= str_buf_size` for unsigned integer types, but sometimes rephrasing an expression can resolve unexpected compiler behavior or type promotion issues, especially in embedded contexts. For `token->len = 10` and `str_buf_size = 10`, `10 > 10 - 1` evaluates to `10 > 9`, which is `true`, thus correctly triggering the `-EINVAL` return. This ensures that strings that are exactly the size of the buffer (leaving no room for the null terminator) are correctly rejected.

